          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-78",
              "name": "Shell command injection via JIRA task ID in coding_workflow.py git/commit/push commands",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Shell command injection via JIRA task ID in coding_workflow.py git/commit/push commands"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The JIRA task ID passed as `sys.argv[1]` to `coding_workflow.py` is interpolated unquoted into several `/bin/bash -c` command strings (`git checkout -b`, `git commit -m`, `git push origin`) that are executed on the AWS Bedrock AgentCore Runtime host via `InvokeAgentRuntimeCommand`. A task ID containing shell metacharacters (spaces, `;`, `$`, quotes) escapes the intended git command and executes arbitrary shell commands on the remote runtime host under the runtime's privileges. The same value is used unquoted as a git branch name and commit message, so option-prefixed values are also interpreted as git options."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The JIRA task ID passed as `sys.argv[1]` to `coding_workflow.py` is interpolated unquoted into several `/bin/bash -c` command strings (`git checkout -b`, `git commit -m`, `git push origin`) that are executed on the AWS Bedrock AgentCore Runtime host via `InvokeAgentRuntimeCommand`. A task ID containing shell metacharacters (spaces, `;`, `$`, quotes) escapes the intended git command and executes arbitrary shell commands on the remote runtime host under the runtime's privileges. The same value is used unquoted as a git branch name and commit message, so option-prefixed values are also interpreted as git options.\n\nArbitrary shell command execution on the AgentCore Runtime host under the runtime's privileges, demonstrated locally by creating a marker file via a task ID payload. The injected commands inherit the `cd /workspace` working directory and ambient IAM credentials of the runtime. The git-push sink is demonstrably attacker-steerable: a locally verified task ID `--receive-pack=touch /tmp/poc/pwned2` makes git spawn the attacker-named program as the remote helper. An attacker who can influence the task ID (a ticket field in an automated trigger, a shared automation queue, or a value copied into the command line) can execute any command on the remote host.\n\nDo not interpolate the task ID into shell strings. Validate it against a strict allowlist (e.g. `^[A-Z]+-\\d+$`) before use, and pass the branch name to git as a discrete argument rather than embedding it in a `bash -c` string — e.g. run `git checkout -b -- <branch>` (with `--` to prevent option interpretation), or better, use a library-level git invocation where the branch name is a single argv element and cannot be tokenized by a shell. Construct the commit message with an explicit argument form as well. If the command string must be assembled, use `shlex.quote()` on every interpolated value — though note `shlex.quote` does not protect the git-option interpretation, hence the `--` separator or allowlist validation is still required.",
                "markdown": "The JIRA task ID passed as `sys.argv[1]` to `coding_workflow.py` is interpolated unquoted into several `/bin/bash -c` command strings (`git checkout -b`, `git commit -m`, `git push origin`) that are executed on the AWS Bedrock AgentCore Runtime host via `InvokeAgentRuntimeCommand`. A task ID containing shell metacharacters (spaces, `;`, `$`, quotes) escapes the intended git command and executes arbitrary shell commands on the remote runtime host under the runtime's privileges. The same value is used unquoted as a git branch name and commit message, so option-prefixed values are also interpreted as git options.\n\nArbitrary shell command execution on the AgentCore Runtime host under the runtime's privileges, demonstrated locally by creating a marker file via a task ID payload. The injected commands inherit the `cd /workspace` working directory and ambient IAM credentials of the runtime. The git-push sink is demonstrably attacker-steerable: a locally verified task ID `--receive-pack=touch /tmp/poc/pwned2` makes git spawn the attacker-named program as the remote helper. An attacker who can influence the task ID (a ticket field in an automated trigger, a shared automation queue, or a value copied into the command line) can execute any command on the remote host.\n\nDo not interpolate the task ID into shell strings. Validate it against a strict allowlist (e.g. `^[A-Z]+-\\d+$`) before use, and pass the branch name to git as a discrete argument rather than embedding it in a `bash -c` string — e.g. run `git checkout -b -- <branch>` (with `--` to prevent option interpretation), or better, use a library-level git invocation where the branch name is a single argv element and cannot be tokenized by a shell. Construct the commit message with an explicit argument form as well. If the command string must be assembled, use `shlex.quote()` on every interpolated value — though note `shlex.quote` does not protect the git-option interpretation, hence the `--` separator or allowlist validation is still required."
              },
