      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-78",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Shell command injection via JIRA task ID in coding_workflow.py git/commit/push commands\n\nThe JIRA task ID passed as `sys.argv[1]` to `coding_workflow.py` is interpolated unquoted into several `/bin/bash -c` command strings (`git checkout -b`, `git commit -m`, `git push origin`) that are executed on the AWS Bedrock AgentCore Runtime host via `InvokeAgentRuntimeCommand`. A task ID containing shell metacharacters (spaces, `;`, `$`, quotes) escapes the intended git command and executes arbitrary shell commands on the remote runtime host under the runtime's privileges. The same value is used unquoted as a git branch name and commit message, so option-prefixed values are also interpreted as git options."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "SECURITY.md"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "/workspace/AgentcoreShell",
                  "kind": "resource"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "b742697919c70513b171eb341c52cc15a451b00b38ee99764d830ffdee37e8ea"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.8",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "12c6db8d05ce36b63d97441c58bd0dc6c7081ec04fd9309c5b5fd83b0f6605fd",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.8,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-21 09:56:12 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/AgentcoreShell",
              "method": "POST",
              "cwe": "CWE-78",
              "impact": "Arbitrary shell command execution on the AgentCore Runtime host under the runtime's privileges, demonstrated locally by creating a marker file via a task ID payload. The injected commands inherit the `cd /workspace` working directory and ambient IAM credentials of the runtime. The git-push sink is demonstrably attacker-steerable: a locally verified task ID `--receive-pack=touch /tmp/poc/pwned2` makes git spawn the attacker-named program as the remote helper. An attacker who can influence the task ID (a ticket field in an automated trigger, a shared automation queue, or a value copied into the command line) can execute any command on the remote host.",
              "technical_analysis": "`coding_workflow.py:103` takes `task` from `sys.argv[1]`. At line 81 it builds `branch = f\"fix/{task.lower()}\"`, then at lines 83, 85-87, and 88-90 interpolates `branch`/`task` raw into `/bin/bash -c \"...\"` command strings passed to `shell.run()`, which sends them to the AgentCore Runtime for execution (`shell_client.py` `run()` → `invoke_agent_runtime_command`). Because the values are placed inside a double-quoted (or single-quoted, for the commit message) bash string with no escaping:\n\n- `;`, `&&`, newlines etc. terminate the git command and start a new one — the classic command-injection primitive, verified locally.\n- `'` in the task ID closes the single-quoted commit message at line 86 and injects shell syntax there too, also verified locally.\n- `$VAR`, backticks and `${IFS}` expand inside the double quotes, useful when spaces are awkward.\n- Since `branch` is `fix/{task}`, a task starting with `-` cannot be a git option for `git checkout -b` (it is prefixed by `fix/`), but git's own refspec/option parsing in `git push origin {branch}` still accepts an embedded `--receive-pack=` style payload after `fix/` because git treats the whole token as `<repository>` and passes it to the remote-helper mechanism — demonstrated locally where git attempted to use the attacker-controlled path as the remote, i.e. it spawns the named program (`--receive-pack=touch /tmp/poc/pwned2` executed `touch`).\n\nNo validation, allowlist, or quoting exists anywhere on the path from `argv` to the shell string.",
              "remediation_steps": "Do not interpolate the task ID into shell strings. Validate it against a strict allowlist (e.g. `^[A-Z]+-\\d+$`) before use, and pass the branch name to git as a discrete argument rather than embedding it in a `bash -c` string — e.g. run `git checkout -b -- <branch>` (with `--` to prevent option interpretation), or better, use a library-level git invocation where the branch name is a single argv element and cannot be tokenized by a shell. Construct the commit message with an explicit argument form as well. If the command string must be assembled, use `shlex.quote()` on every interpolated value — though note `shlex.quote` does not protect the git-option interpretation, hence the `--` separator or allowlist validation is still required.",
              "counterevidence": "Actively looked for mitigations: no input validation, allowlist, regex, or sanitization of `task` anywhere in the file or the repository; `shell.run` passes the string verbatim to the runtime with no filtering; no allowlist of git subcommand arguments and no `--` end-of-options guard before the interpolated branch name. The only mitigating factor is that the operator launches the script — if every invocation is a human typing a fully trusted task ID, the injection requires a compromised or careless invocation, which lowers exposure but does not constitute a control.",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Evidence that these scripts are invoked with task IDs sourced from an untrusted or automated channel (ticketing webhook, queue consumer, CI parameter) would raise this to critical; conversely, if the tool is confirmed to be used only interactively by a single trusted operator who always types task IDs by hand, severity could be argued down to medium.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Stub `boto3` (no AWS contact) and substitute `AgentCoreShell` with a local mock whose `run()` executes the exact command string via `subprocess` in a scratch git repo, simulating the runtime host.\n2. Call `coding_workflow(mock_shell, \"x; touch /tmp/poc/pwned #\")` — the same code path `main()` drives with `sys.argv[1]`.\n3. Observe the interpolated command `/bin/bash -c \"cd /workspace && git checkout -b fix/x; touch /tmp/poc/pwned #\"` and that the marker file is created on the executing host.\n4. Separately, run the step-5 command with a task ID of `'; touch /tmp/poc/pwned3; x='` to show the commit-message sink also escapes quoting, and with `--receive-pack=touch /tmp/poc/pwned2` to show git option injection in `git push origin {branch}`.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/hardcoded-secret-credential-exposure",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hardcoded secret / credential exposure — No issue identified: config.py hardcoded AgentCore runtime ARN / AWS account ID 632930644527\n\ngitleaks (worktree + single-commit git history, gitleaks.json empty) and trufflehog (0 findings) both clean. The value is a resource ARN containing the account ID and agent name — an identifier, not a credential; cannot authenticate or authorize anything. Informational exposure at most, per task instruction not a reportable vulnerability."
          },
          "locations": [
