        {
          "ruleId": "strix-coverage/shell-quote-breaking-on-user-typed-commands",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "kind": "pass",
          "level": "none",
          "message": {
            "text": "Shell quote-breaking on user-typed commands — Ruled out: interactive.py:50 f'/bin/bash -c \"{cmd}\"'\n\nControl: intended operation. The REPL's documented purpose is to execute whatever the local operator types on the runtime host (threat model: \"Command execution via the REPL is the intended product, not a finding\"). Quote-breaking (a typed `\"` in the command breaks the wrapper and shifts argument boundaries — verified locally that `echo \"hello\"; touch /tmp/poc/pwned4 #` runs the semicolon segment) only changes what the operator already fully controls, so it is not an exploitable boundary here. Documented as a robustness/hygiene issue, not a vulnerability report."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "interactive.py:50 f'/bin/bash -c \"{cmd}\"'"
                }
              ]
